/* Probability Top-1% Trap Compendium - generated by tools/build_content.py */
window.trapData = [{"id":"T1","title":"The unconventional path (read this first)","part":"Trap compendium","codes":[],"lede":"The traditional path is theory → solved examples → exercises → mocks. It takes 14–18 months and produces students who “know” probability but still lose 15–20 questions to traps. The inverted path used by toppers:","groups":[{"title":"The inverted path","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Traps first.** Learn every statement in this compendium as a *true/false object*, with the counterexample attached. MCQs are built from exactly these fault lines.","k":"key"},{"x":"**PYQs as the syllabus.** Solve ISS Statistics-I previous-year questions (2016–2025) for probability *before* finishing theory. Also mine IIT JAM (MS), GATE (ST), and CSIR–NET Part B/C for question patterns: these three exams share question-setters’ culture with ISS.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Counterexample flashcards.** Ninety percent of “assertion–reason” and “which of the following is/are correct” items die instantly if you carry a mental deck of about 40 counterexamples (all included below, marked TRAP).","k":"key"},{"x":"**Error ledger.** Every mistake goes into a ledger with the trap-class it belongs to. You revise *the ledger*, not the textbook.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Speed arithmetic of guessing.** With 4 options, $+2.5$ for right, $-0.833$ for wrong: random guessing has expected value $0.25(2.5)-0.75(0.833)=0$ (neutral); eliminating even *one* option makes guessing strictly profitable: $\\tfrac13(2.5)-\\tfrac23(0.833)\\approx+0.28$. Top scorers never leave a question blank once one option is eliminated.","k":"key"}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T2","title":"Classical and axiomatic definitions: where MCQs hide","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P1"],"lede":"Foundations are examined as true/false statements about the exact strength of the axioms, the direction of each inequality, and the many ways independence is misread.","groups":[{"title":"Classical (Laplace) definition: the four attack points","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$P(A)=\\dfrac{\\text{favourable cases}}{\\text{total cases}}$ *only if* outcomes are finite **and** equally likely. **MCQ angle:** “Probability that a natural number chosen at random is even” — ill-posed classically (infinite sample space); needs limiting (natural density) arguments."},{"x":"The definition is **circular**: “equally likely” already means “equally probable”. A favourite one-liner for assertion–reason items.","k":"trap"},{"x":"It cannot handle unequally likely outcomes (biased coin) or continuous sample spaces. Geometric probability (favourable measure/total measure) extends it, but then Bertrand’s chord paradox shows the extension is *not unique*.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Classical probability is automatically a valid probability measure (it satisfies Kolmogorov’s axioms); the converse direction is what fails."}]}]},{"title":"Kolmogorov’s axioms and their exact strength","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"On a sample space $\\Omega$ with a $\\sigma$-field $\\mathcal F$: (A1) $P(A)\\ge0$; (A2) $P(\\Omega)=1$; (A3) **countable** additivity: $P\\left(\\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^\\infty P(A_i)$ for pairwise disjoint $A_i$."},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"The axiom is *countable* additivity, not finite, and not “additivity over arbitrary (uncountable) unions”. Uncountable additivity is **false**: for $X\\sim U(0,1)$, $1=P(\\Omega)\\ne\\sum_{x\\in[0,1]}P(\\{x\\})=0$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Countable additivity $\\iff$ finite additivity + continuity from above at $\\varnothing$ (if $A_n\\downarrow\\varnothing$ then $P(A_n)\\to0$). **MCQ angle:** “Which additional condition upgrades finite to countable additivity?”","k":"key"},{"x":"Consequences you must be able to *derive*, since MCQs test the direction of each inequality: $P(\\varnothing)=0$; $P(A^c)=1-P(A)$; monotonicity $A\\subseteq B\\Rightarrow P(A)\\le P(B)$; $P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)$; inclusion–exclusion for $n$ events; Boole: $P(\\bigcup A_i)\\le\\sum P(A_i)$; Bonferroni: $P(A\\cap B)\\ge P(A)+P(B)-1$, and generally $P(\\bigcap_{i=1}^nA_i)\\ge\\sum P(A_i)-(n-1)$."},{"x":"Continuity of probability: $A_n\\uparrow A\\Rightarrow P(A_n)\\uparrow P(A)$ and $A_n\\downarrow A\\Rightarrow P(A_n)\\downarrow P(A)$. Continuity from above needs no extra condition here because $P$ is finite; in general measure theory it needs $\\mu(A_1)<\\infty$ — a favourite “statistics vs. measure” trick statement.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$P(A)=0$ does **not** imply $A=\\varnothing$ (a continuous variable hits any fixed point with probability 0 yet hits *some* point). $P(A)=1$ does not imply $A=\\Omega$. Language: “almost surely”.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$P(A)=P(B)$ does not imply $A=B$; $P(A\\cap B)=0$ does not imply $A\\cap B=\\varnothing$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"The domain matters: $P$ is defined on a $\\sigma$-field (closed under complements and *countable* unions), not on all subsets of $\\Omega$ in general. **MCQ angle:** “The smallest $\\sigma$-field containing $A$” is $\\{\\varnothing,A,A^c,\\Omega\\}$ (4 sets); the smallest $\\sigma$-field containing two “generic” events $A,B$ has 16 sets; a $\\sigma$-field over a finite $\\Omega$ always has $2^k$ members for some $k$ — never 3, 5, or 6 events. A quick kill: “Can a $\\sigma$-field contain exactly 6 sets?” No."}]}]},{"title":"Independence: the single richest MCQ mine in foundations","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Definition: $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$. It is a property of the *measure*, not of the sets."},{"x":"**Mutually exclusive $\\ne$ independent.** Disjoint events with $P(A)>0$, $P(B)>0$ are *never* independent ($0\\ne P(A)P(B)>0$). They are, in fact, maximally dependent: knowing $A$ happened kills $B$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Any event with probability 0 or 1 is independent of *every* event, including itself. $A$ independent of itself $\\iff P(A)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ (since $P(A)=P(A)^2$)."},{"x":"If $A\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp B$ then automatically $A\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp B^c$, $A^c\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp B$, $A^c\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp B^c$."},{"x":"**Pairwise independence $\\not\\Rightarrow$ mutual independence.** Bernstein’s example: toss two fair coins; $A=\\{\\text{first is head}\\}$, $B=\\{\\text{second is head}\\}$, $C=\\{\\text{exactly one head}\\}$. Each pair is independent ($P=\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12$), but $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)=0\\ne\\tfrac18$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Conversely, $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)=P(A)P(B)P(C)$ alone does *not* give pairwise independence. Mutual independence of $n$ events requires the product rule for **every** sub-collection: $2^n-n-1$ equations.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Independence is *not* transitive: $A\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp B$, $B\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp C$ does not give $A\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp C$."},{"x":"Conditional independence neither implies nor is implied by independence. Classic: two coins, one fair and one two-headed, pick one at random; successive tosses are conditionally independent given the coin but unconditionally dependent.","k":"trap"}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T3","title":"Conditional probability, total probability, Bayes: the paradox belt","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P2"],"lede":"Conditional-probability items are ordinary identities wearing a costume, or a paradox whose verdict you must know by heart.","groups":[{"title":"Machinery with trap annotations","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$P(A\\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}$, defined **only when** $P(B)>0$. **MCQ angle:** Statements quietly conditioning on probability-zero events are automatically suspect (see Borel–Kolmogorov below)."},{"x":"For fixed $B$, the map $A\\mapsto P(A\\mid B)$ is itself a full probability measure — so *every* identity (complement rule, inclusion–exclusion, Bayes) holds with an extra “$\\mid B$” everywhere. Many MCQs are ordinary identities wearing a conditional costume.","k":"key"},{"x":"$P(A\\mid B)+P(A\\mid B^c)$ need **not** equal 1. What equals 1 is $P(A\\mid B)+P(A^c\\mid B)$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"No general order between $P(A\\mid B)$ and $P(A)$: conditioning can raise, lower, or preserve probability. “$B$ occurred, so $A$ is now more likely” is not a theorem.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Multiplication rule: $P(A_1\\cap\\cdots\\cap A_n)=P(A_1)P(A_2\\mid A_1)\\cdots P(A_n\\mid A_1\\cdots A_{n-1})$ — the backbone of urn/without-replacement questions."},{"x":"Law of total probability: for a partition $\\{B_i\\}$ with $P(B_i)>0$: $P(A)=\\sum_iP(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)$. The $B_i$ must be exhaustive *and* mutually exclusive; examiners drop one of the two conditions in “which assumptions are needed” items.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Bayes: $P(B_j\\mid A)=\\dfrac{P(A\\mid B_j)P(B_j)}{\\sum_iP(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)}$. Confusing $P(A\\mid B)$ with $P(B\\mid A)$ is the *prosecutor’s fallacy* — tested through medical-test and forensic wordings.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Symmetry principle** for sampling without replacement: the unconditional probability that the $k$-th draw is red equals the proportion of reds initially, *for every $k$*. (Pólya urn versions also appear.) This one line solves a whole family of “second ball” questions in 10 seconds.","k":"key"}]}]},{"title":"The paradox arsenal (each is a ready-made 2027 MCQ)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Base-rate / false-positive paradox.** Disease prevalence 0.1%; test sensitivity 99%, specificity 99%. $P(\\text{disease}\\mid+)=\\tfrac{0.99\\times0.001}{0.99\\times0.001+0.01\\times0.999}\\approx9\\%$, *not* 99%. When the prior is tiny, even excellent tests give mostly false positives.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Monty Hall.** Three doors, host *knowingly* opens a goat door; switching wins with probability $\\tfrac23$. If the host opens a door at random and it merely *happens* to show a goat, switching wins with probability $\\tfrac12$. The host’s knowledge is the entire problem. (100-door version: switching wins $\\tfrac{99}{100}$.)","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Boy–girl paradox (order of information).** Two children, equally likely sexes. Given “at least one boy”: $P(\\text{both boys})=\\tfrac13$. Given “the elder is a boy”: $\\tfrac12$. Given “at least one boy born on a Tuesday”: $\\tfrac{13}{27}$. *How* you learned the information changes the conditioning event.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Bertrand’s box paradox.** Three boxes: GG, SS, GS. A random coin from a random box is gold; $P(\\text{other coin gold})=\\tfrac23$, not $\\tfrac12$ (count *coins*, not boxes)."},{"x":"**Bertrand’s chord paradox.** “A random chord of a circle is longer than the side of the inscribed equilateral triangle” has answers $\\tfrac13$ (random endpoints), $\\tfrac12$ (random midpoint on a fixed radius), $\\tfrac14$ (random midpoint in the disc). Moral: “at random” in continuous settings is meaningless until the randomising mechanism is fixed — the standard MCQ asks you to match method to answer.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Simpson’s paradox.** Treatment A can beat B inside *every* subgroup yet lose on aggregate, because group sizes act as hidden weights. **MCQ angle:** It is possible that $P(S\\mid A\\cap C)>P(S\\mid B\\cap C)$ and $P(S\\mid A\\cap C^c)>P(S\\mid B\\cap C^c)$, yet $P(S\\mid A)<P(S\\mid B)$ — **True** (the two treatments meet the subgroups with different weights). But with a *common* partition and common weights, $P(A\\mid C)>P(B\\mid C)$ and $P(A\\mid C^c)>P(B\\mid C^c)$ *do* force $P(A)>P(B)$ — the exam plays these two lookalike statements against each other.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Gambler’s fallacy.** Independent tosses have no memory: after 10 heads, $P(\\text{head})$ is still $\\tfrac12$. Contrast deliberately with memorylessness of geometric/exponential (which is a property of those laws, not of “luck”)."},{"x":"**Two-envelope paradox.** The bogus computation $\\mathbb{E}(\\text{other})=\\tfrac12(2x)+\\tfrac12(x/2)=1.25x$ illegally treats $x$ as fixed under both hypotheses / assumes an improper uniform prior on all amounts. **MCQ angle:** “Where does the argument break?”"},{"x":"**Borel–Kolmogorov paradox.** Conditional distributions given a probability-zero event (a point on a great circle of a uniformly random point on a sphere) are not intrinsically defined: different limiting parametrisations give different answers. **Conditioning must be on a $\\sigma$-field/partition, not on an isolated null event.**"},{"x":"**de Méré’s paradox.** At least one six in 4 throws: $1-(5/6)^4\\approx0.518>\\tfrac12$; at least one double-six in 24 double throws: $1-(35/36)^{24}\\approx0.491<\\tfrac12$. Naive proportionality ($4/6$ vs $24/36$) fails."},{"x":"**Birthday paradox.** 23 people suffice for $P(\\text{shared birthday})>\\tfrac12$ ($\\approx0.507$). The related but different question “someone shares *my* birthday” needs 253 people — examiners swap the two.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**St. Petersburg paradox.** Payoff $2^n$ with probability $2^{-n}$: expectation $=\\infty$ although the game is “obviously” worth little; shows the mean may fail to exist/be infinite and cannot always guide decisions."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T4","title":"Random variables and distribution functions: fine print","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P3"],"lede":"Distribution-function items test the exact properties of a CDF, the strict-vs-weak inequality distinctions, and the “which of these is a CDF” battery.","groups":[{"title":"Distribution function (CDF): the exact properties","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"$F(x)=P(X\\le x)$ satisfies, and is characterised by: (i) non-decreasing; (ii) right-continuous; (iii) $F(-\\infty)=0$, $F(+\\infty)=1$. Conversely, every function with (i)–(iii) is the CDF of some random variable (correspondence theorem)."},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Right-continuous, not necessarily left-continuous. The jump at $x$ is exactly the point mass: $P(X=x)=F(x)-F(x^-)$. Also $P(X<x)=F(x^-)$, $P(a<X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a)$, $P(a\\le X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a^-)$. Examiners live inside these strict-vs-weak inequality distinctions.","k":"trap"},{"x":"A CDF has at most countably many discontinuities (jumps of size $\\ge1/n$ number at most $n$). **MCQ angle:** “Can a CDF have uncountably many jumps?” No."},{"x":"“$F$ continuous $\\Rightarrow$ density exists” is **false**. The Cantor (Lebesgue singular) distribution has a continuous CDF, no point masses, yet no density: $F'=0$ almost everywhere while $F$ climbs from 0 to 1. Lebesgue decomposition: every CDF = mixture of discrete + absolutely continuous + singular continuous parts. “Continuous random variable” in exam usage means absolutely continuous (density exists) — but a top-1% item can hinge on the distinction.","k":"trap"},{"x":"A pdf may exceed 1 (e.g. $U(0,\\tfrac12)$ has $f=2$; Beta$(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$ is even unbounded). Only $f\\ge0$ and $\\int f=1$ are required. Contrast: a pmf can never exceed 1.","k":"trap"},{"x":"A pdf is not unique — altering it on a set of measure zero changes nothing. And $f$ need not be continuous or bounded."},{"x":"Mixed distributions exist and are examinable: e.g. $F$ with a jump at 0 and a density on $(0,\\infty)$ (rainfall; waiting time with $P(X=0)>0$). For these, neither pure $\\sum$ nor pure $\\int$ computes moments alone."},{"x":"For continuous $F$: $P(X=x)=0$ for every $x$ — yet $X$ always takes some value. “Probability zero” $\\ne$ “impossible”."},{"x":"Symmetry about 0 (continuous case): $F(-x)=1-F(x)$; median $=0$; all existing odd moments vanish; the characteristic function is real."},{"x":"Quantiles: $Q(p)=\\inf\\{x:F(x)\\ge p\\}$. The median need not be unique (any point of a flat stretch of $F$ at level $\\tfrac12$); conventionally the midpoint is quoted.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"Building new CDFs from old: a favourite “which of these is a CDF” battery","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Candidate","CDF?","Reason / interpretation"],"rows":[["$\\alpha F+(1-\\alpha)G$, $0\\le\\alpha\\le1$","Yes","Mixture distribution"],["$FG$","Yes","CDF of $\\max(X,Y)$, $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y$"],["$F^n$ ($n\\in\\mathbb N$)","Yes","Max of $n$ iid copies"],["$1-(1-F)^n$","Yes","Min of $n$ iid copies"],["$F+G$","No","Tends to 2"],["$F(ax+b)$, $a>0$","Yes","Location–scale change"],["$F(ax+b)$, $a<0$","No","Decreasing"],["$F(G(x))$","**No in general**","Limits are $F(0)\\ge0$ and $F(1)\\le1$, not 0, 1; valid iff $F$ puts all mass on $[0,1]$ (e.g. uniform/Beta)"],["$1-F(-x^-)$","Yes","CDF of $-X$"]]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Test the three axioms mechanically; interpret when possible (max/min interpretations also produce direct questions on order statistics).","k":"key"}]}]},{"title":"Random variables: definitional traps","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"A random variable is a (measurable) function $\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$; it is neither random nor a variable — assertion–reason bait."},{"x":"A constant is a legitimate random variable (degenerate distribution; CDF is a unit step). Its variance is 0, and $\\mathrm{Var}(X)=0\\iff X$ is a.s. constant."},{"x":"Two different random variables can have the same distribution without being equal: $X\\sim N(0,1)$ and $-X$ have identical laws but $P(X=-X)=P(X=0)=0$. “Equal in distribution” $\\ne$ “equal”. MCQs phrase this as “$X\\overset{d}{=}Y\\Rightarrow X=Y$?” — False.","k":"trap"},{"x":"If $X$ has CDF $F$ continuous, then $F(X)\\sim U(0,1)$ (probability integral transform); conversely $F^{-1}(U)$ has CDF $F$ (inverse transform / simulation). Continuity of $F$ is essential for the first direction; the second works for any $F$ via the generalised inverse.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Existence of moments is a property of the tail of $F$. Heavier tail $\\Rightarrow$ fewer moments. The Cauchy has none (not even the mean); $t$-type tails with index $\\alpha$ have moments of order $<\\alpha$ only."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T5","title":"Standard distributions: distribution-by-distribution ambush points","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P4","P5"],"lede":"For every family memorise five hooks: (support, mean, variance, generating function, one *characterising* property). The characterising property is what the top-1% items test.","groups":[{"title":"Bernoulli(p)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$\\mathbb{E}X=p$, $\\mathrm{Var}X=pq\\le\\tfrac14$, maximised at $p=\\tfrac12$. **MCQ angle:** “Largest possible variance of a 0–1 variable” $=\\tfrac14$."},{"x":"$X^k=X$ for all $k\\ge1$: every raw moment equals $p$. A one-line MCQ."},{"x":"Indicator calculus: $\\mathbf 1_{A\\cap B}=\\mathbf 1_A\\mathbf 1_B$, $\\mathbf 1_{A\\cup B}=\\mathbf 1_A+\\mathbf 1_B-\\mathbf 1_A\\mathbf 1_B$; expectation of an indicator is a probability — the trick behind “matching/coupon” expectation items."}]}]},{"title":"Binomial(n, p)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"MGF $(q+pe^t)^n$; PGF $(q+ps)^n$. Mean $np$ **always exceeds** variance $npq$ (underdispersion). **MCQ angle:** “mean $=8$, variance $=16$” is impossible for a binomial — instant elimination."},{"x":"If mean $np=a$ and variance $npq=b$ then $q=b/a$, $p=1-b/a$, $n=a^2/(a-b)$; $n$ must come out a positive integer, else no such binomial exists."},{"x":"Mode: $\\lfloor(n+1)p\\rfloor$; if $(n+1)p$ is an integer, *two* modes: $(n+1)p$ and $(n+1)p-1$. Recurrence $\\tfrac{P(X=k+1)}{P(X=k)}=\\tfrac{n-k}{k+1}\\cdot\\tfrac pq$."},{"x":"Additivity: $\\mathrm{Bin}(m,p)+\\mathrm{Bin}(n,p)=\\mathrm{Bin}(m+n,p)$ for independent summands — *only with common $p$*. Different $p$’s give a non-binomial law.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Symmetric iff $p=\\tfrac12$. $n-X\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}(n,q)$."},{"x":"Limits: $n\\to\\infty$, $np\\to\\lambda$: Poisson (law of rare events); fixed $p$, large $n$: normal (de Moivre–Laplace) with continuity correction $P(X\\le k)\\approx\\Phi\\left(\\tfrac{k+0.5-np}{\\sqrt{npq}}\\right)$. **MCQ angle:** Poisson approximation is good when $n$ large, $p$ small; normal when $npq$ large."}]}]},{"title":"Poisson(λ)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Mean = variance $=\\lambda$: the *equidispersion* signature. Factorial moments $\\mathbb{E}X(X-1)\\cdots(X-r+1)=\\lambda^r$."},{"x":"Mode $\\lfloor\\lambda\\rfloor$; two modes $\\lambda-1,\\lambda$ when $\\lambda$ is a positive integer. Recurrence $P(X=k+1)=\\tfrac{\\lambda}{k+1}P(X=k)$."},{"x":"PGF $e^{\\lambda(s-1)}$; MGF $e^{\\lambda(e^t-1)}$. Additive: independent Poissons sum to Poisson (rates add). **Raikov’s theorem** (converse, top-1%): if $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y$, both non-degenerate, and $X+Y$ is Poisson, then $X$ and $Y$ are each Poisson.","k":"key"},{"x":"The *difference* of independent Poissons is **not** Poisson (it can be negative; it is Skellam). Constant multiples $cX$ ($c\\ne1$) are not Poisson either — “$2X$ is Poisson with mean $2\\lambda$” is False.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Conditional: $X\\mid X+Y=n\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}\\left(n,\\tfrac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}\\right)$ — a recurring exam favourite.","k":"key"},{"x":"Thinning: independently keeping each event with probability $p$ turns Poisson$(\\lambda)$ into Poisson$(p\\lambda)$; the kept and discarded streams are *independent* (surprising — classic top-end MCQ)."},{"x":"$P(X\\text{ even})=e^{-\\lambda}\\cosh\\lambda=\\tfrac12(1+e^{-2\\lambda})>\\tfrac12$."},{"x":"Poisson $\\to$ Normal as $\\lambda\\to\\infty$ (standardised); continuity correction applies."}]}]},{"title":"Geometric(p): the discrete memoryless law","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Two conventions.** Trials to first success, support $\\{1,2,\\dots\\}$: $P(X=k)=q^{k-1}p$, $\\mathbb{E}X=1/p$, $\\mathrm{Var}X=q/p^2$, $P(X>k)=q^k$. Failures before first success, support $\\{0,1,\\dots\\}$: $\\mathbb{E}Y=q/p$, same variance. Check the support before answering *any* numeric item.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Memorylessness $P(X>m+n\\mid X>m)=P(X>n)$ characterises the geometric among discrete laws on $\\{1,2,\\dots\\}$ (and the exponential among continuous laws). “The only memoryless discrete distribution” — True (with that support)."},{"x":"$\\min(X_1,X_2)$ of independent geometrics is geometric with $q=q_1q_2$."},{"x":"Mode is always the smallest support point (1 or 0); the pmf is strictly decreasing."}]}]},{"title":"Uniform / Rectangular","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Two objects share the name.** *Discrete* uniform on $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$: mean $\\tfrac{n+1}2$, variance $\\tfrac{n^2-1}{12}$. *Continuous* (rectangular) $U(a,b)$: mean $\\tfrac{a+b}2$, variance $\\tfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}$. The two variance formulas are deliberately confusable.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$U(a,b)$: MGF exists everywhere: $\\tfrac{e^{bt}-e^{at}}{(b-a)t}$ ($t\\ne0$); all moments finite. No unique mode."},{"x":"Sum of two iid $U(0,1)$ is **triangular** on $(0,2)$ — not uniform. $X+Y$ uniform is impossible for iid $X,Y$ (no distribution convolves with itself to a uniform).","k":"trap"},{"x":"$-\\ln U\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(1)$; $F^{-1}(U)$ simulates $F$; $F(X)\\sim U(0,1)$ for continuous $F$ (probability integral transform)."},{"x":"Order statistics: $U_{(k)}\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$; $\\mathbb{E}U_{(k)}=\\tfrac{k}{n+1}$; range $R=U_{(n)}-U_{(1)}\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(n-1,2)$."},{"x":"Maximum entropy on a bounded interval."}]}]},{"title":"Exponential(λ): the continuous memoryless law","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Mean $1/\\lambda$, variance $1/\\lambda^2$, median $\\tfrac{\\ln2}{\\lambda}\\approx0.693/\\lambda<$ mean; $P(X>\\mathbb{E}X)=e^{-1}\\approx0.368$ (not $\\tfrac12$ — a beloved trap). Mode $=0$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Memorylessness characterises it among continuous laws on $(0,\\infty)$; equivalently constant hazard rate $\\lambda$. “Used/unused component equally good” wordings."},{"x":"$\\min(X_1,\\dots,X_n)\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda_1+\\cdots+\\lambda_n)$; $P(X_1<X_2)=\\tfrac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}$; the minimum and the index achieving it are *independent* (top-1% fact).","k":"key"},{"x":"Sums are *not* exponential: $\\sum_{i=1}^n$ iid $\\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)=\\mathrm{Gamma/Erlang}(n,\\lambda)$. The *max* of iid exponentials is not exponential either.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$X_1-X_2$ (iid) $\\sim$ Laplace; $2\\lambda X\\sim\\chi^2_2$; interarrival times of a Poisson process are iid exponential — the bridge fact."},{"x":"Maximum entropy on $(0,\\infty)$ given the mean."}]}]},{"title":"Normal(μ, σ²)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"MGF $e^{\\mu t+\\sigma^2t^2/2}$ (exists everywhere); mean = median = mode; inflection points at $\\mu\\pm\\sigma$; central moments: odd all 0, $\\mathbb{E}(X-\\mu)^{2k}=\\sigma^{2k}(2k-1)!!$, so $\\mathbb{E}(X-\\mu)^4=3\\sigma^4$ (kurtosis 3, excess 0)."},{"x":"Coverage: $\\mu\\pm\\sigma$: 68.27%, $\\mu\\pm2\\sigma$: 95.45%, $\\mu\\pm3\\sigma$: 99.73%. Mean deviation $=\\sigma\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\approx0.7979\\sigma$; QD $\\approx0.6745\\sigma$; QD : MD : SD $\\approx10:12:15$."},{"x":"Any linear combination of independent normals is normal: $aX+bY\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2,a^2\\sigma_1^2+b^2\\sigma_2^2)$. Variances add with $a^2,b^2$ — for $X-Y$ the variance is $\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2$ (never subtract).","k":"trap"},{"x":"$X$ normal, $Y$ normal $\\not\\Rightarrow X+Y$ normal, unless independent or jointly normal. Counterexample: $Y=X\\cdot S$ with $S=\\pm1$ a fair sign flip independent of $X\\sim N(0,1)$: $Y\\sim N(0,1)$, but $X+Y$ has an atom at 0 ($P(X+Y=0)=\\tfrac12$), so it is not normal. Same example shows: marginally normal, uncorrelated, yet dependent — “uncorrelated normals are independent” is False without joint normality.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Characterisations (top-1% material): Cramér: if $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y$ and $X+Y$ is normal, each is normal. Bernstein: if $X,Y$ iid and $X+Y\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp X-Y$, then they are normal. For iid $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$, $X+Y$ and $X-Y$ are independent — special to the normal. Maximum entropy for fixed variance. $\\bar X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp S^2$ iff normal.","k":"key"},{"x":"$Z^2\\sim\\chi^2_1=\\mathrm{Gamma}(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$; $X/Y\\sim$ Cauchy for iid $N(0,1)$; $\\lvert Z\\rvert$ is half-normal with $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert Z\\rvert=\\sqrt{2/\\pi}$; $\\bar X_n\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2/n)$ exactly, at every $n$."},{"x":"$\\Phi(-x)=1-\\Phi(x)$; standardisation $Z=(X-\\mu)/\\sigma$ — sign errors here are the cheapest marks lost in the exam.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"Cauchy(θ, σ): the professional counterexample","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Density $\\tfrac1\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sigma}{\\sigma^2+(x-\\theta)^2}$. **No mean** (the defining integral diverges: undefined, not “0 by symmetry” — a razor-sharp trap), hence no variance, no higher moments, no MGF at any $t\\ne0$. Median = mode $=\\theta$; quartiles $\\theta\\pm\\sigma$; use quartile deviation, not SD.","k":"trap"},{"x":"CF: $\\phi(t)=e^{i\\theta t-\\sigma\\lvert t\\rvert}$ — exists (CFs always do), but is not differentiable at 0, matching the nonexistent mean."},{"x":"$\\bar X_n\\sim\\mathrm{Cauchy}(\\theta,\\sigma)$ for every $n$: averaging does nothing. Hence WLLN, SLLN and CLT all fail; the sample median, not the sample mean, estimates $\\theta$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Stable family: independent Cauchys add to a Cauchy (location and scale both add). $1/X$, $\\tan U$ for $U\\sim U(-\\tfrac\\pi2,\\tfrac\\pi2)$, and the ratio of iid $N(0,1)$ are standard Cauchy; Cauchy $=t$-distribution with 1 degree of freedom."}]}]},{"title":"Hypergeometric(N, K, n)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Sampling without replacement: draws are dependent. Mean $n\\tfrac KN$ (same as binomial); variance $n\\tfrac KN\\left(1-\\tfrac KN\\right)\\tfrac{N-n}{N-1}$ — binomial variance shrunk by the finite population correction $\\tfrac{N-n}{N-1}<1$. **MCQ angle:** “Same mean, smaller variance than the matching binomial” — True."},{"x":"As $N\\to\\infty$ with $K/N\\to p$: converges to $\\mathrm{Bin}(n,p)$ — justifies treating draws from huge populations as independent."},{"x":"By symmetry the unconditional law of each single draw is Bernoulli$(K/N)$ even without replacement — independence fails, identical distribution does not.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"Multinomial(n; p₁, …, p_k)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Marginals: $X_i\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}(n,p_i)$. Components are **negatively** correlated by the constraint $\\sum X_i=n$: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_i,X_j)=-np_ip_j$, $\\rho_{ij}=-\\sqrt{\\tfrac{p_ip_j}{(1-p_i)(1-p_j)}}$ — the sign question is a standard MCQ.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Conditionals stay in the family: $X_i\\mid X_j=m\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}\\left(n-m,\\tfrac{p_i}{1-p_j}\\right)$; lumping cells keeps it multinomial."},{"x":"The joint pmf coefficient is $\\binom n{x_1\\cdots x_k}=\\tfrac{n!}{x_1!\\cdots x_k!}$; for $k=2$ it collapses to the binomial — “binomial is a multinomial with $k=2$”."}]}]},{"title":"Laplace (double exponential)(μ, b)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Density $\\tfrac1{2b}e^{-\\lvert x-\\mu\\rvert/b}$: a symmetric peak (not smooth) at $\\mu$. Mean = median = mode $=\\mu$; variance $2b^2$ (not $b^2$); kurtosis 6 (excess 3): sharply leptokurtic — “more peaked and heavier-tailed than normal”.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$X_1-X_2$ for iid exponentials is Laplace — the constructive definition MCQs use."},{"x":"CF $\\tfrac{e^{i\\mu t}}{1+b^2t^2}$; MGF exists only for $\\lvert t\\rvert<1/b$."},{"x":"MLE of $\\mu$ is the sample median (contrast: normal $\\to$ mean, uniform $\\to$ midrange) — a three-way matching question waiting to happen."}]}]},{"title":"Negative Binomial(r, p)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Two conventions again.** Trials until $r$-th success $X$ (support $\\{r,r+1,\\dots\\}$): $\\mathbb{E}X=r/p$, $\\mathrm{Var}=rq/p^2$. Failures before $r$-th success $Y$ (support $\\{0,1,\\dots\\}$): $\\mathbb{E}Y=rq/p$, $\\mathrm{Var}=rq/p^2$. Variance $>$ mean (overdispersion) — the mirror image of the binomial; the “mean vs variance” triage (binomial $<$, Poisson $=$, negative binomial $>$) is a guaranteed MCQ.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Sum of $r$ iid geometrics; closed under independent addition with common $p$ (add the $r$’s)."},{"x":"Gamma-mixed Poisson is negative binomial — the standard model for overdispersed counts (top-1% link fact)."},{"x":"Limit: $r\\to\\infty$, $q\\to0$, $rq\\to\\lambda$ gives Poisson$(\\lambda)$."}]}]},{"title":"Beta(α, β)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Support $(0,1)$; mean $\\tfrac\\alpha{\\alpha+\\beta}$; variance $\\tfrac{\\alpha\\beta}{(\\alpha+\\beta)^2(\\alpha+\\beta+1)}$; mode $\\tfrac{\\alpha-1}{\\alpha+\\beta-2}$ for $\\alpha,\\beta>1$."},{"x":"Shape triage: $\\alpha=\\beta$ symmetric; both $<1$: U-shaped (arcsine Beta$(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$ has unbounded density — densities need not be bounded); Beta$(1,1)=U(0,1)$."},{"x":"$1-X\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(\\beta,\\alpha)$; uniform order statistics $U_{(k)}\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$."},{"x":"Gamma bridge: for independent Gamma$(\\alpha_1,\\lambda)$, Gamma$(\\alpha_2,\\lambda)$: $\\tfrac X{X+Y}\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2)$ and is independent of $X+Y$ (Lukacs’ characterisation of the gamma — elite MCQ material)."}]}]},{"title":"Gamma(α, λ) (shape–rate)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Mean $\\alpha/\\lambda$, variance $\\alpha/\\lambda^2$, MGF $(1-t/\\lambda)^{-\\alpha}$ valid **only for $t<\\lambda$** — “MGF exists for all $t$” is False. Skewness $2/\\sqrt\\alpha\\to0$: gamma $\\to$ normal as $\\alpha\\to\\infty$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Shape–rate vs shape–scale parametrisations differ by $\\lambda\\leftrightarrow1/\\theta$; check which one the question uses before computing anything.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Additivity in the shape parameter for common rate: Gamma$(\\alpha_1,\\lambda)+$Gamma$(\\alpha_2,\\lambda)=$ Gamma$(\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2,\\lambda)$. Special cases: exponential $=$ Gamma$(1,\\lambda)$; $\\chi^2_k=$ Gamma$(k/2,\\tfrac12)$."},{"x":"Scaling: $cX\\sim\\mathrm{Gamma}(\\alpha,\\lambda/c)$ — shape is scale-invariant."},{"x":"Mode $\\tfrac{\\alpha-1}{\\lambda}$ for $\\alpha\\ge1$; for $\\alpha<1$ the density blows up at 0."}]}]},{"title":"Lognormal(μ, σ²)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$X=e^Z$, $Z\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$. $\\mu,\\sigma^2$ are the mean/variance of $\\ln X$, **not** of $X$. Median $e^\\mu$; mean $e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}$; mode $e^{\\mu-\\sigma^2}$; always mode $<$ median $<$ mean (right skew).","k":"trap"},{"x":"All moments finite: $\\mathbb{E}X^k=e^{k\\mu+k^2\\sigma^2/2}$ — yet the MGF does not exist for any $t>0$. “All moments exist $\\Rightarrow$ MGF exists” is False; the lognormal is the canonical counterexample.","k":"trap"},{"x":"The lognormal is not determined by its moments: distinct distributions share all its moments (moment problem indeterminacy). “Equal moments of all orders $\\Rightarrow$ equal distributions” is False in general (true if an MGF exists in a neighbourhood of 0).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Products (not sums) of independent lognormals are lognormal; multiplicative CLT: products of many positive iid factors are asymptotically lognormal."}]}]},{"title":"Cross-family cheat tables (matching-type questions)","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Question type","Answer set"],"rows":[["Closed under independent summation","Binomial (common $p$), Poisson, Normal, Gamma (common rate; includes $\\chi^2$), Cauchy, Negative binomial (common $p$)"],["Not closed","Uniform (sum triangular), Exponential (sum Gamma), Geometric (sum NB), Bernoulli (sum Binomial), Beta, Laplace, Hypergeometric, Lognormal (products instead)"],["Limit lattice","Hypergeometric $\\to$ Binomial ($N\\to\\infty$); Binomial $\\to$ Poisson ($n\\to\\infty$, $np\\to\\lambda$); Binomial $\\to$ Normal (de Moivre–Laplace); Poisson $\\to$ Normal ($\\lambda\\to\\infty$); Negative binomial $\\to$ Poisson ($r\\to\\infty$, $rq\\to\\lambda$); Gamma $\\to$ Normal ($\\alpha\\to\\infty$); Geometric (rescaled) $\\to$ Exponential; $t_\\nu\\to$ Normal ($\\nu\\to\\infty$) with $t_1=$ Cauchy"],["Mean vs variance triage (count data)","Binomial: Var $<$ Mean; Poisson: Var $=$ Mean; Negative binomial: Var $>$ Mean"],["Memorylessness","Geometric (only discrete), Exponential (only continuous). No other distribution qualifies — any MCQ claiming otherwise is False"],["Maximum entropy","Bounded support $\\to$ Uniform; positive with fixed mean $\\to$ Exponential; fixed mean and variance on $\\mathbb R$ $\\to$ Normal"],["MGF existence","Everywhere: Normal, Binomial, Poisson, Uniform, Bernoulli; in a neighbourhood only: Exponential/Gamma ($t<\\lambda$), Laplace ($\\lvert t\\rvert<1/b$), Geometric/NB ($e^t<1/q$); nowhere useful: Cauchy (no $t\\ne0$), Lognormal (no $t>0$). CFs exist for all of them, always"]]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T6","title":"Random vectors: joint, marginal, conditional and transformations","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P6","P7"],"lede":"The joint law is a one-way street to its marginals; the exam rewards the support check, the independence hierarchy and the exact form of every change-of-variable rule.","groups":[{"title":"Joint vs marginal: the one-way street","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"The joint distribution determines all marginals; marginals **never** determine the joint (except under independence). Canonical counterexample: both $(X,Y)$ bivariate normal with $\\rho=0$, and the “sign-flipped” pair $(X,SX)$, have standard normal marginals with entirely different joint laws. “Standard normal marginals $\\Rightarrow$ bivariate normal” is **False**.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Joint CDF $F(x,y)$ properties: non-decreasing and right-continuous in each argument, $F(-\\infty,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$, $F(\\infty,\\infty)=1$, *plus* the rectangle inequality $F(b,d)-F(a,d)-F(b,c)+F(a,c)\\ge0$. The rectangle condition is the one candidates forget — monotone-in-each-argument alone is *not* sufficient to be a joint CDF (e.g. $F(x,y)=\\min(1,\\max(0,x+y))$-type examples fail it).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Marginal extraction: $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty)$; $f_X(x)=\\int f(x,y)\\,dy$. Conditional density $f_{Y\\mid X}(y\\mid x)=f(x,y)/f_X(x)$ for $f_X(x)>0$ — it is a genuine density in $y$ (integrates to 1) — and conditioning on $\\{X=x\\}$, a null event, is defined through this density, not through the elementary formula (Borel–Kolmogorov again)."}]}]},{"title":"Independence of random variables: precision points","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Definition: $F(x,y)=F_X(x)F_Y(y)$ for **all** $(x,y)$; equivalently the density/pmf factorises for (almost) all points."},{"x":"**Factorisation shortcut:** if $f(x,y)=g(x)h(y)$ on a **rectangular** support (product set), then $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y$ (no need to normalise $g,h$).","k":"key"},{"x":"If the support is *not* a product set — e.g. $f(x,y)=2$ on the triangle $0<x<y<1$, or uniform on a disc — the variables are dependent *no matter what* the formula looks like. Support check first, always.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Uniform on the disc $x^2+y^2\\le1$: $X,Y$ uncorrelated but dependent (the range of $Y$ depends on $X$). Uniform on the *square*: independent. A ready-made pair of MCQ options."},{"x":"$X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\Rightarrow g(X)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp h(Y)$ for any (measurable) $g,h$. The converse direction of specific instances fails; and $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y$, $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Z$ do **not** give $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp(Y,Z)$ or $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y+Z$ in general.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Uncorrelated $\\ne$ independent: $X\\sim N(0,1)$, $Y=X^2$: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb{E}X^3=0$, yet $Y$ is a function of $X$. Independence $\\Rightarrow$ uncorrelated (when moments exist); the converse holds inside a *jointly* normal vector — that “jointly” is the entire MCQ.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"Distributions of functions of random variables","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Monotone change of variable: $Y=g(X)$, $g$ strictly monotone with inverse $g^{-1}$: $f_Y(y)=f_X\\left(g^{-1}(y)\\right)\\left\\lvert\\tfrac{d}{dy}g^{-1}(y)\\right\\rvert$. Forgetting the absolute value, or using $dg/dx$ instead of $dg^{-1}/dy$, are the two standard planted wrong options.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Non-monotone: $Y=X^2$: $f_Y(y)=\\tfrac1{2\\sqrt y}\\left[f_X(\\sqrt y)+f_X(-\\sqrt y)\\right]$, $y>0$. With $X\\sim N(0,1)$ this gives $\\chi^2_1$ — derive it once, remember it forever."},{"x":"Bivariate (Jacobian) method: $(U,V)=T(X,Y)$ invertible: $f_{U,V}(u,v)=f_{X,Y}\\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)\\lvert J\\rvert$ where $J=\\det\\tfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$. The Jacobian of the *inverse* map appears; equivalently $\\lvert J\\rvert=1/\\lvert J_{\\text{forward}}\\rvert$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Convolution: $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\Rightarrow f_{X+Y}(z)=\\int f_X(x)f_Y(z-x)\\,dx$. Get limits from the supports; for positive variables the integral runs over $0<x<z$ only.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Standard function-of-variable identities (memorise as a table): $Z^2\\sim\\chi^2_1$; $X/Y\\sim$ Cauchy (iid $N(0,1)$); $-\\tfrac1\\lambda\\ln U\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$; $F(X)\\sim U(0,1)$; $\\tfrac{X}{X+Y}\\sim$ Beta (independent gammas, common rate), independent of $X+Y$; min of exponentials exponential; $X_1-X_2$ of iid exponentials Laplace; Box–Muller: $\\sqrt{-2\\ln U_1}\\cos(2\\pi U_2)$ and $\\sqrt{-2\\ln U_1}\\sin(2\\pi U_2)$ are iid $N(0,1)$.","k":"key"},{"x":"Order statistics (iid, CDF $F$, density $f$): $F_{X_{(n)}}=F^n$, $F_{X_{(1)}}=1-(1-F)^n$; $f_{X_{(k)}}(x)=\\tfrac{n!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}F^{k-1}(1-F)^{n-k}f(x)$. Max and min of iid variables are **not** independent, and the identity $X_{(1)}+X_{(n)}\\overset{d}{=}X_1+X_n$ is false in general.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$\\mathbb{E}g(X)$ via the law of the unconscious statistician: no need to find the law of $g(X)$ first — but $\\mathbb{E}g(X)\\ne g(\\mathbb{E}X)$ in general (Jensen direction: $\\ge$ for convex $g$, $\\le$ for concave)."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T7","title":"Modes of convergence: the counterexample arsenal","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P10"],"lede":"Convergence questions are answered by one picture and four counterexamples; the rest is the exact quantifier in each definition.","groups":[{"title":"The hierarchy (memorise as a picture)","blocks":[{"t":"fig","x":"conv"},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"No other implication holds in general. **Two special upgrades:** (i) $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}c$ (a **constant**) $\\Rightarrow X_n\\xrightarrow{P}c$; (ii) $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}X\\Rightarrow$ some *subsequence* converges a.s. Also, a.s. convergence and $L^2$ convergence do not imply each other.","k":"key"}]}]},{"title":"Definitions with the exact quantifier traps","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"In probability: $\\forall\\varepsilon>0$, $P(\\lvert X_n-X\\rvert>\\varepsilon)\\to0$. The probability of a *single* large deviation dies."},{"x":"Almost surely: $P(\\{\\omega:X_n(\\omega)\\to X(\\omega)\\})=1$. The entire *path* converges, except on a null set. Equivalent tail form: $P(\\sup_{k\\ge n}\\lvert X_k-X\\rvert>\\varepsilon)\\to0$."},{"x":"In mean square: $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X_n-X\\rvert^2\\to0$ (needs second moments). $L^r$ convergence $\\Rightarrow L^s$ for $s<r$ (Lyapunov), and $\\Rightarrow$ convergence of $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X_n\\rvert^r\\to\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X\\rvert^r$."},{"x":"In distribution: $F_n(x)\\to F(x)$ at every **continuity point** of $F$. Convergence at discontinuity points is *not* required: $X_n=1/n$ (degenerate) $\\xrightarrow{d}0$, yet $F_n(0)=0\\not\\to F(0)=1$. This is a certified classic MCQ.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Convergence in distribution is a statement about *laws*, not values: $X_n$ and $X$ need not even live on the same probability space, and $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$, $Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}Y$ does **not** imply $X_n+Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}X+Y$ (unless e.g. independence throughout, or one limit is constant — Slutsky).","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"The four counterexamples that answer 90% of convergence MCQs","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Typewriter / sliding blocks** ($\\xrightarrow{P}$ and $\\xrightarrow{L^2}$ but *not* a.s.): on $[0,1]$ with Lebesgue measure, let $X_n$ be indicators of intervals of shrinking length $1/k$ sweeping repeatedly across $[0,1]$. Then $P(X_n\\ne0)\\to0$ so $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}0$ and $\\mathbb{E}X_n^2\\to0$, but every $\\omega$ is hit infinitely often: $X_n(\\omega)$ does not converge *anywhere*.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Tall spike** ($\\xrightarrow{a.s.}$ and $\\xrightarrow{P}$ but *not* in mean square): $X_n=n\\,\\mathbf 1_{(0,1/n)}(U)$, $U\\sim U(0,1)$. Then $X_n\\to0$ a.s. (for each $\\omega$, eventually $U>1/n$), but $\\mathbb{E}X_n=1$ for all $n$ and $\\mathbb{E}X_n^2=n\\to\\infty$: no $L^1$ or $L^2$ convergence. Moral: convergence a.s. says nothing about moments — “$X_n\\xrightarrow{a.s.}X\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}X_n\\to\\mathbb{E}X$” is False (needs uniform integrability or dominated convergence).","k":"key"},{"x":"**Independent rare events** ($\\xrightarrow{P}$ but not a.s.): independent $X_n\\in\\{0,1\\}$ with $P(X_n=1)=1/n$. Then $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}0$; but $\\sum1/n=\\infty$ and independence, so by Borel–Cantelli II $X_n=1$ infinitely often a.s.: no a.s. limit. With $P(X_n=1)=1/n^2$ instead, $\\sum<\\infty$: Borel–Cantelli I gives $X_n\\xrightarrow{a.s.}0$. The exponent 1 vs 2 is exactly where the MCQ lives.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Symmetric flip** ($\\xrightarrow{d}$ but not $\\xrightarrow{P}$): $X\\sim N(0,1)$ and $X_n=-X$ for all $n$. Then $X_n\\overset{d}{=}X$ so trivially $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$, but $\\lvert X_n-X\\rvert=2\\lvert X\\rvert$ never shrinks. Convergence of distributions is not convergence of random variables.","k":"key"}]}]},{"title":"Theorems the examiner expects you to wield in one line","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Slutsky:** $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$, $Y_n\\xrightarrow{P}c\\Rightarrow X_n+Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}X+c$, $X_nY_n\\xrightarrow{d}cX$, $X_n/Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}X/c$ ($c\\ne0$). $Y_n$ must tend to a *constant*, not a random limit.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Continuous mapping:** $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X\\Rightarrow g(X_n)\\xrightarrow{d}g(X)$ for $g$ continuous (a.s. w.r.t. the limit law); same statement for $\\xrightarrow{P}$ and $\\xrightarrow{a.s.}$."},{"x":"**Pólya:** if $F_n\\to F$ pointwise and $F$ is continuous, the convergence is automatically uniform in $x$ — an elegant true statement often planted among false ones."},{"x":"**Scheffé:** pointwise convergence of densities $\\Rightarrow$ convergence in distribution (indeed in total variation). The converse fails: $\\xrightarrow{d}$ does not imply density convergence (densities may not even exist).","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Monotone relationships:** $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}X$ and $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}Y\\Rightarrow X=Y$ a.s. (limits in probability are a.s. unique). Limits in distribution are unique only as laws."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T8","title":"Expectation and conditional expectation","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P8"],"lede":"Every expectation item is an existence question, a linearity/Jensen identity, or one of the seven laws of conditional expectation.","groups":[{"title":"Existence and computation traps","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$\\mathbb{E}X$ exists (finitely) iff $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X\\rvert<\\infty$.","k":"key"},{"x":"For the Cauchy, $\\int_{-a}^ax f(x)\\,dx=0$ for every $a$, yet the mean is undefined — symmetry is not permission. “Cauchy has mean 0 by symmetry” is the single most planted false option in this chapter.","k":"trap"},{"x":"St. Petersburg: payoff $2^n$ w.p. $2^{-n}$ ($n=1,2,\\dots$): $\\mathbb{E}X=\\sum1=\\infty$. Infinite expectations are legal; the LLN conclusion then reads $\\bar X_n\\to\\infty$ a.s."},{"x":"Existence ladder (Lyapunov): $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X\\rvert^r<\\infty\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X\\rvert^s<\\infty$ for all $0<s<r$. Higher moments imply lower — the reverse claim is **False** ($t_3$ has a mean and variance but no third moment).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Tail formulas: $X\\ge0$: $\\mathbb{E}X=\\int_0^\\infty P(X>x)\\,dx$; integer-valued: $\\mathbb{E}X=\\sum_{k\\ge1}P(X\\ge k)$. These crack “expected number of trials” MCQs in seconds.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Linearity is unconditional:** $\\mathbb{E}(aX+bY)=a\\mathbb{E}X+b\\mathbb{E}Y$ requires no independence whatsoever. Half of all “expected number of matches/records” items are solved by indicators + linearity: e.g. expected number of fixed points of a random permutation $=1$, for every $n$.","k":"key"},{"x":"Products: $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}(XY)=\\mathbb{E}X\\,\\mathbb{E}Y$. Converse false (uncorrelated $\\ne$ independent). And $\\mathbb{E}(X/Y)\\ne\\mathbb{E}X/\\mathbb{E}Y$; $\\mathbb{E}(1/X)\\ne1/\\mathbb{E}X$ — Jensen gives $\\mathbb{E}(1/X)\\ge1/\\mathbb{E}X$ for positive $X$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Jensen: $g$ convex $\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}g(X)\\ge g(\\mathbb{E}X)$ (reverse for concave; equality iff $g$ affine on the support or $X$ degenerate). Consequences: $\\mathbb{E}X^2\\ge(\\mathbb{E}X)^2$ (i.e. $\\mathrm{Var}\\ge0$); $\\mathbb{E}e^{tX}\\ge e^{t\\mathbb{E}X}$; $\\mathbb{E}\\ln X\\le\\ln\\mathbb{E}X$."},{"x":"Cauchy–Schwarz: $(\\mathbb{E}XY)^2\\le\\mathbb{E}X^2\\,\\mathbb{E}Y^2$; equality iff linear dependence a.s. This is also why $\\lvert\\rho\\rvert\\le1$ and $\\lvert\\rho\\rvert=1$ iff $Y=aX+b$."},{"x":"Variance identities: $\\mathrm{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\mathrm{Var}X$ (the “$+b$” vanishes, $a$ is squared — so $\\mathrm{Var}(-X)=\\mathrm{Var}X$); $\\mathrm{Var}(X\\pm Y)=\\mathrm{Var}X+\\mathrm{Var}Y\\pm2\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$ — variances add for the difference of uncorrelated variables. Cov is bilinear; $\\mathrm{Cov}(X,c)=0$; correlation is invariant under positive affine maps and flips sign under negative scaling.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"Conditional expectation: the seven laws","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"$\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]$ is a **random variable** (a function of $Y$), not a number. MCQs that treat it as a constant are auto-false.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Tower / total expectation:** $\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]\\right]=\\mathbb{E}X$. **Total variance:** $\\mathrm{Var}X=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{Var}(X\\mid Y)]+\\mathrm{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y])$ — both terms $\\ge0$, hence conditioning never increases expected residual variance: $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{Var}(X\\mid Y)]\\le\\mathrm{Var}X$.","k":"key"},{"x":"Taking out what is known: $\\mathbb{E}[g(Y)X\\mid Y]=g(Y)\\,\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]$."},{"x":"Independence: $X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]=\\mathbb{E}X$. Converse false: $\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]=\\mathbb{E}X$ (mean independence) does not force independence (e.g. $X=YZ$ with symmetric $Z$). Hierarchy: independence $\\Rightarrow$ mean independence $\\Rightarrow$ uncorrelated; both arrows strict.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Best predictor: $\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]$ minimises $\\mathbb{E}(X-g(Y))^2$ over all $g$ — the regression function. The best *linear* predictor is generally different (they coincide under joint normality — another “jointly” trap).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Symmetry trick: for iid $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ and $S=\\sum X_i$: $\\mathbb{E}[X_1\\mid S]=S/n$. Instant answer to a recurring exam item.","k":"key"},{"x":"Standard-model conditionals to memorise: Poisson split $X\\mid X+Y\\sim$ Binomial; multinomial cell given another cell $\\sim$ Binomial; bivariate normal: $\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=\\mu_Y+\\rho\\tfrac{\\sigma_Y}{\\sigma_X}(X-\\mu_X)$ (linear!) with constant conditional variance $\\sigma_Y^2(1-\\rho^2)$."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T9","title":"Generating functions: CF, MGF, PGF: inversion, uniqueness, continuity","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P9"],"lede":"The characteristic function always exists; the MGF may not; the three big theorems (uniqueness, inversion, Lévy continuity) are won by stating their hypotheses exactly.","groups":[{"title":"Characteristic functions: what is always true","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"$\\varphi_X(t)=\\mathbb{E}e^{itX}$."},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Exists for **every** distribution (since $\\lvert e^{itX}\\rvert=1$) — the decisive advantage over the MGF. Properties: $\\varphi(0)=1$; $\\lvert\\varphi(t)\\rvert\\le1$; uniformly continuous on $\\mathbb R$; Hermitian $\\varphi(-t)=\\overline{\\varphi(t)}$; $\\varphi_{aX+b}(t)=e^{ibt}\\varphi_X(at)$.","k":"key"},{"x":"$\\varphi$ is real-valued $\\iff X$ is symmetric about 0. MCQ angle: “$\\varphi$ real $\\Rightarrow$ mean 0” — False (Cauchy: real CF, no mean).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Independence: $\\varphi_{X+Y}=\\varphi_X\\varphi_Y$. The converse is **False**: for $X$ standard Cauchy and $Y=X$ (maximally dependent), $\\varphi_{X+Y}(t)=e^{-2\\lvert t\\rvert}=\\varphi_X(t)\\varphi_Y(t)$. Factorisation of the CF does not certify independence.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Not every nice function is a CF: $\\lvert\\varphi(t)\\rvert\\le1$ with $\\varphi(0)=1$ is necessary, not sufficient (Bochner’s theorem gives the full criterion: non-negative definiteness). E.g. $e^{-t^4}$ is *not* a CF (a genuine top-1% item: if it were, the fourth-moment/kurtosis relations force a degenerate–normal contradiction).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Moments: if $\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X\\rvert^n<\\infty$ then $\\varphi^{(n)}(0)=i^n\\mathbb{E}X^n$; in particular $\\varphi'(0)=i\\mathbb{E}X$, $\\varphi''(0)=-\\mathbb{E}X^2$. Differentiability of $\\varphi$ at 0 does **not** guarantee the mean exists (one-way street for odd orders; existence of $\\varphi^{(2k)}(0)$ *does* force $\\mathbb{E}X^{2k}<\\infty$ — even orders are special).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Landmark CFs: $N(0,1)$: $e^{-t^2/2}$ (self-conjugate shape); Cauchy: $e^{-\\lvert t\\rvert}$; Poisson: $e^{\\lambda(e^{it}-1)}$; $U(-1,1)$: $\\tfrac{\\sin t}{t}$; Laplace$(0,1)$: $\\tfrac1{1+t^2}$; degenerate at $c$: $e^{ict}$."}]}]},{"title":"The three big theorems (statement precision wins the mark)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Uniqueness:** $\\varphi_X=\\varphi_Y$ (for all $t$) $\\iff X\\overset{d}{=}Y$. CFs are fingerprints. Agreement on an interval is *not* enough in general.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Inversion:** for continuity points $a<b$: $P(a<X\\le b)=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\tfrac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-T}^T\\tfrac{e^{-ita}-e^{-itb}}{it}\\varphi(t)\\,dt$; if $\\int\\lvert\\varphi\\rvert<\\infty$, $X$ has the bounded continuous density $f(x)=\\tfrac1{2\\pi}\\int e^{-itx}\\varphi(t)\\,dt$. **MCQ angle:** “integrable CF $\\Rightarrow$ density exists” — True; converse False.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Lévy continuity:** $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X\\iff\\varphi_n(t)\\to\\varphi(t)$ for every $t$ with $\\varphi$ a CF; equivalently the pointwise limit is **continuous at 0**. The continuity-at-0 clause is the exam’s favourite: $X_n\\sim N(0,n)$ gives $\\varphi_n(t)=e^{-nt^2/2}\\to\\mathbf 1\\{t=0\\}$, discontinuous at 0 — and indeed the mass escapes to $\\pm\\infty$, no limiting distribution. This single example is worth a full mark every time it appears.","k":"trap"}]}]},{"title":"MGF and PGF: existence fine print","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"MGF $M(t)=\\mathbb{E}e^{tX}$ may fail to exist for all $t\\ne0$ (Cauchy) or all $t>0$ (lognormal). If $M$ exists on a neighbourhood $(-\\delta,\\delta)$: all moments are finite, $M^{(k)}(0)=\\mathbb{E}X^k$, and $M$ **determines the distribution**."},{"x":"“All moments finite $\\Rightarrow$ distribution determined by moments” is **False** (lognormal). “MGF exists near 0 $\\Rightarrow$ determined” is True. Keep the two statements separated in memory; the exam will put them side by side.","k":"trap"},{"x":"$M_{X+Y}=M_XM_Y$ under independence (same caveat as CFs on the converse); $M_{aX+b}(t)=e^{bt}M_X(at)$; $M(0)=1$ always; $M$ is convex; $M(t)\\ge e^{t\\mathbb{E}X}$ (Jensen)."},{"x":"PGF (non-negative integer variables): $G(s)=\\mathbb{E}s^X=\\sum p_ks^k$, radius $\\ge1$; $G(1)=1$; $p_k=G^{(k)}(0)/k!$ (*probabilities* from derivatives at 0); factorial moments from derivatives at 1: $G'(1)=\\mathbb{E}X$, $G''(1)=\\mathbb{E}X(X-1)$, so $\\mathrm{Var}X=G''(1)+G'(1)-[G'(1)]^2$ — this formula is asked directly.","k":"key"},{"x":"$G_{X+Y}=G_XG_Y$ (independent); random sums: $\\mathbb{E}s^{S_N}=G_N(G_X(s))$ (compound/branching PGF) with $\\mathbb{E}S_N=\\mathbb{E}N\\,\\mathbb{E}X$ (Wald) — top-1% but appears in “number of offspring”-style items."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T10","title":"Zero–one laws and the classical inequalities","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P11","P12"],"lede":"Borel–Cantelli and Kolmogorov’s 0–1 law fix a probability at 0 or 1 (never which one); Markov, Chebyshev and Kolmogorov bound it with exact hypotheses.","groups":[{"title":"Borel–Cantelli and Borel’s 0–1 law","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**BC-I:** $\\sum P(A_n)<\\infty\\Rightarrow P(A_n\\text{ i.o.})=0$. Needs *no* independence — planted MCQs claim it does.","k":"key"},{"x":"**BC-II:** if the $A_n$ are independent and $\\sum P(A_n)=\\infty$, then $P(A_n\\text{ i.o.})=1$. Independence here is essential: take $A_n=A$ with $P(A)=\\tfrac12$ for all $n$; the sum diverges but $P(A_n\\text{ i.o.})=\\tfrac12$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Borel’s 0–1 law:** for independent events, $P(A_n\\text{ i.o.})$ is 0 or 1 according as $\\sum P(A_n)$ converges or diverges — no middle value possible. Infinite-monkey corollary: any fixed finite pattern in iid typing occurs infinitely often with probability 1."},{"x":"i.o. notation: $\\{A_n\\text{ i.o.}\\}=\\limsup A_n=\\bigcap_n\\bigcup_{k\\ge n}A_k$; $\\liminf A_n=\\{A_n\\text{ eventually always}\\}$; $\\liminf A_n\\subseteq\\limsup A_n$; $P(\\liminf A_n)\\le\\liminf P(A_n)\\le\\limsup P(A_n)\\le P(\\limsup A_n)$ (Fatou for sets) — the inequality chain itself is an MCQ."}]}]},{"title":"Kolmogorov’s 0–1 law","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"For an independent sequence $X_1,X_2,\\dots$, every **tail event** (unchanged by altering finitely many $X_i$’s) has probability 0 or 1."},{"x":"Tail events (probability forced to $\\{0,1\\}$): $\\{\\sum X_n\\text{ converges}\\}$, $\\{\\lim X_n\\text{ exists}\\}$, $\\{\\limsup X_n>c\\}$, $\\{X_n\\in B\\text{ i.o.}\\}$, $\\{\\bar X_n\\text{ converges}\\}$. Non-tail (trap options): $\\{X_1>0\\}$, $\\{\\sum X_n>0\\}$, $\\{S_n=0\\text{ i.o.}\\}$ — these depend on early terms.","k":"trap"},{"x":"The law says the probability is 0 or 1; it does **not** tell you which. Determining which one is the hard analytic work (e.g. via Borel–Cantelli or the three-series theorem).","k":"trap"},{"x":"Consequences: $\\limsup X_n$, $\\limsup S_n/n$ etc. are a.s. constants (possibly $\\pm\\infty$) for independent sequences."}]}]},{"title":"Tchebycheff, Markov, Kolmogorov: exact hypotheses, exact bounds","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Markov:** $X\\ge0$, $a>0$: $P(X\\ge a)\\le\\mathbb{E}X/a$. Non-negativity is essential; the bound is useless for $a\\le\\mathbb{E}X$ (bound $\\ge1$).","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Tchebycheff:** $P(\\lvert X-\\mu\\rvert\\ge k\\sigma)\\le1/k^2$, equivalently $P(\\lvert X-\\mu\\rvert\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\sigma^2/\\varepsilon^2$. Needs finite variance, no distributional shape. **MCQ angle:** complement form $P(\\lvert X-\\mu\\rvert<k\\sigma)\\ge1-1/k^2$ (at least 75% within $2\\sigma$, 88.9% within $3\\sigma$ — compare with 95.45%, 99.73% for the normal: “universal vs normal” pairs are repeatedly asked).","k":"key"},{"x":"Tchebycheff is **sharp**: $X=\\pm k\\sigma$ w.p. $\\tfrac1{2k^2}$ each and 0 w.p. $1-\\tfrac1{k^2}$ attains equality — so “the inequality can be improved without further assumptions” is False. One-sided (Cantelli): $P(X-\\mu\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{\\sigma^2+\\varepsilon^2}$ — strictly better on one side.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Kolmogorov’s inequality:** independent, mean-zero, finite-variance $X_i$, $S_k=\\sum_{i\\le k}X_i$: $P\\left(\\max_{1\\le k\\le n}\\lvert S_k\\rvert\\ge\\varepsilon\\right)\\le\\tfrac{\\mathrm{Var}(S_n)}{\\varepsilon^2}$. It bounds the maximum of all partial sums by the same quantity Tchebycheff uses for $S_n$ alone — “Kolmogorov strengthens Tchebycheff” is the expected true statement; it is the engine of the SLLN proof.","k":"key"}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T11","title":"Laws of large numbers and central limit theorems","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P13"],"lede":"LLN and CLT items are hypothesis bookkeeping: iid? finite mean? finite variance? independence? standardisation? The marks sit in the hypotheses.","groups":[{"title":"WLLN vs SLLN: hypothesis bookkeeping (pure MCQ fuel)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Tchebycheff/Markov WLLN** (not nec. identically distributed): pairwise uncorrelated with $\\mathrm{Var}(S_n)/n^2\\to0$ (e.g. uniformly bounded variances) $\\Rightarrow\\bar X_n-\\mathbb{E}\\bar X_n\\xrightarrow{P}0$. Needs variances."},{"x":"**Khinchin’s WLLN:** iid with finite mean $\\Rightarrow\\bar X_n\\xrightarrow{P}\\mu$. **No variance assumption.** “WLLN requires finite variance” — False (for iid); this is among the most re-asked discriminator items.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Kolmogorov’s SLLN (iid):** $\\bar X_n\\xrightarrow{a.s.}\\mu\\iff\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X_1\\rvert<\\infty$ (and then $\\mu=\\mathbb{E}X_1$). Finite mean is necessary and sufficient.","k":"key"},{"x":"**Kolmogorov’s SLLN (independent, non-iid):** $\\sum_n\\mathrm{Var}(X_n)/n^2<\\infty\\Rightarrow\\tfrac{S_n-\\mathbb{E}S_n}{n}\\xrightarrow{a.s.}0$ (Kolmogorov’s criterion). Note the $n^2$ in the denominator — MCQs swap it with $n$.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Cauchy kills everything: iid Cauchy has $\\bar X_n\\sim$ Cauchy for all $n$: no WLLN, no SLLN, no CLT. The St. Petersburg variable (infinite mean) gives $\\bar X_n\\to\\infty$. “LLN holds for every iid sequence” — False; finite mean is the gatekeeper.","k":"trap"},{"x":"SLLN $\\Rightarrow$ WLLN (a.s. implies in probability). A sequence can obey the WLLN and violate the SLLN (independent, suitably weighted examples) — direction matters."},{"x":"Bernoulli LLN: relative frequency $\\xrightarrow{P}p$ — the formal bridge between the frequency interpretation and the axioms; also $\\xrightarrow{a.s.}$ by Borel’s SLLN (1909), historically the first strong law."}]}]},{"title":"CLT: the three named versions","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Lindeberg–Lévy (iid):** iid, $\\mathbb{E}X=\\mu$, $\\mathrm{Var}X=\\sigma^2\\in(0,\\infty)$: $\\tfrac{\\sqrt n(\\bar X_n-\\mu)}{\\sigma}\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1)$. Both moments finite is the only distributional assumption — no symmetry, no continuity. Finite variance is essential: iid Cauchy fails; iid Pareto with infinite variance converges to stable, non-normal, laws.","k":"key"},{"x":"**de Moivre–Laplace:** the binomial special case, $\\tfrac{X-np}{\\sqrt{npq}}\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1)$; with continuity correction $P(X\\le k)\\approx\\Phi\\left(\\tfrac{k+0.5-np}{\\sqrt{npq}}\\right)$ — expect a numeric MCQ where the 0.5 decides between two options."},{"x":"**Lyapunov** (independent, non-iid): if for some $\\delta>0$ $\\tfrac{\\sum_i\\mathbb{E}\\lvert X_i-\\mu_i\\rvert^{2+\\delta}}{s_n^{2+\\delta}}\\to0$ (with $s_n^2=\\sum\\mathrm{Var}X_i$), then $\\tfrac{S_n-\\mathbb{E}S_n}{s_n}\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1)$. Lyapunov’s condition is *sufficient*, not necessary; the sharp iff-type condition (given uniform negligibility) is **Lindeberg–Feller** — knowing which name carries the “necessary and sufficient” label is a top-1% discriminator.","k":"trap"},{"x":"CLT vs LLN division of labour: LLN says $\\bar X_n\\to\\mu$; CLT says the error is of exact order $n^{-1/2}$ with a universal Gaussian shape: $\\bar X_n\\approx N(\\mu,\\sigma^2/n)$. Berry–Esseen quantifies the speed: $\\sup_x\\lvert F_n(x)-\\Phi(x)\\rvert\\le C\\rho/(\\sigma^3\\sqrt n)$ (order $n^{-1/2}$, needs a third moment)."},{"x":"The CLT is about the *standardised* sum: $S_n$ itself diverges, and $\\bar X_n$ collapses to a constant; neither is asymptotically normal without the $\\sqrt n$ scaling — sloppy MCQ stems exploit this.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Proof-sketch fact examiners quote: CF method — $\\varphi_{S_n/\\sqrt n}(t)=\\left(1-\\tfrac{t^2}{2n}+o(1/n)\\right)^n\\to e^{-t^2/2}$, then Lévy continuity. Know this two-line skeleton; “which theorem finishes the CF proof of the CLT?” has appeared in several exams’ orbit."}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T12","title":"The two 66-item verdict banks","part":"Trap compendium","codes":["P1","P2","P3","P4","P5","P6","P7","P8","P9","P10","P11","P12","P13"],"lede":"The compendium’s §12 “expected-question bank” holds every trap as a true/false statement with its verdict. Decide first, then check. Score 58–60 (of the printed 60): exam-ready on theory traps; 52–57: re-read the matching sections and retest in 48 hours; below 52: restart the compendium with the counterexamples first.","groups":[{"title":"Practise the bank","blocks":[{"t":"launch","bank":"tf-trap","x":"Start the 66 trap statements (Learn or Exam)"},{"t":"launch","bank":"tf-notes","x":"Start the 66-item notes drill (Learn or Exam)"},{"t":"key","x":"Anything you miss goes into the **error ledger**: in this dashboard every wrong answer is saved automatically in your Revise tab, tagged with its trap class."}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T13","title":"The mistakes that waste 80% of beginners’ time","part":"Trap compendium","codes":[],"lede":"The same fourteen mistakes account for most lost marks. Read them as a checklist before every mock.","groups":[{"title":"The fourteen mistakes","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Reading theory before PYQs.** You cannot recognise a trap you have never been cut by. Invert: questions first, theory as the debrief.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Deriving in the exam what should be a lookup.** Modes, memorylessness, closure-under-addition, mean/variance of all 16 families, the convergence hierarchy: these must be reflexes (under 10 seconds), not derivations. Every derivation in the hall is 2 minutes stolen from 3 other questions.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Learning distributions as isolated formulas instead of as a **network** (limit lattice, closure table, characterisations). Matching questions are set on the network, not the formulas.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Ignoring parametrisation conventions.** Geometric (from 0 or 1), negative binomial (trials or failures), gamma (rate or scale), exponential ($\\lambda$ or mean $\\theta$): more marks die here than on any genuinely hard idea.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Believing symmetry authorises expectations.** The Cauchy mean does not exist. Full stop.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Treating a.s. convergence and mean-square convergence as ordered.** They are not comparable; keep the two counterexamples loaded.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Confusing pairwise with mutual independence, and uncorrelatedness with independence.** Three different notions, strictly ordered only one way.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Forgetting the support.** Non-rectangular support kills independence regardless of the formula; convolution limits come from supports; conditional densities exist only where the marginal is positive.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Skipping the “jointly” in jointly normal.** Marginal normality is almost never enough for anything.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Over-practising integration, under-practising verdicts.** ISS objective papers reward 90-second true/false discrimination, not 15-minute integrals. Allocate practice in that proportion.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**No error ledger.** Unlogged mistakes recur with probability $\\approx1$ (a very strong law). The ledger, revised weekly, is the highest-ROI hour of the week.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Leaving eliminate-one questions blank.** Expected value of a guess after one elimination is positive ($\\approx+0.28$ marks). Blind blanks are a scoring error, not caution.","k":"trap"},{"x":"**Mock-hoarding.** Twenty analysed mocks beat sixty unanalysed ones. The analysis (trap-class tagging) is the practice.","k":"trap"},{"x":"Studying limit theorems as folklore (“everything becomes normal”) instead of as precise statements with hypotheses. The marks sit in the hypotheses: iid?, finite mean?, finite variance?, independence?, standardisation?","k":"trap"}]}]}],"src":"traps"},{"id":"T14","title":"The aggressive 2027 practice plan","part":"Trap compendium","codes":[],"lede":"Assumption: ISS 2027 written exam around mid-2027 (UPSC typically notifies in February and examines in June). This plan covers the Probability block of Statistics-I; run parallel tracks for the other blocks with the same architecture.","groups":[{"title":"Phase 0 — Calibration (weeks 1–2)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Attempt every probability question from ISS Statistics-I PYQs 2020–2025, untimed, closed book. Record: correct / wrong / lucky / blank."},{"x":"Tag every error with a trap-class from this compendium (e.g. “support”, “parametrisation”, “hierarchy”, “hypothesis of theorem”). This tagging is the diagnosis; it tells you which sections here to over-weight."},{"x":"Set up the error ledger (one line per error: date, source, trap-class, the one-sentence reason, retest date +7d and +30d). *In this dashboard the Revise tab is that ledger.*"}]}]},{"title":"Phase 1 — Trap-first mastery (weeks 3–14; the engine room)","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Six blocks, two weeks each, in this order (dependency-sorted): (1) Foundations + conditioning/Bayes; (2) RVs + CDFs + univariate transformations; (3) The 16 distributions (one family per morning, cross-tables at week end); (4) Random vectors + Jacobians + order statistics; (5) Expectation + conditional expectation + CF/MGF/PGF; (6) Convergence + inequalities + 0–1 laws + LLN/CLT."},{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"**Daily rhythm (2.5–3 h):** 30 min: read the matching compendium section actively (cover the verdict, predict, check)."},{"x":"75 min: 25–35 MCQs on that block. Sources in priority order: ISS PYQs (2016–2025), IIT JAM (MS) PYQs, GATE (ST) PYQs, CSIR–NET Statistics Part B — these share the ISS question-setting culture; standard drill books (Gupta–Kapoor for volume; Rohatgi–Saleh and Casella–Berger exercises for the top-1% edge) fill gaps."},{"x":"20 min: ledger entries + flashcards (every TRAP line in this file is one card)."},{"x":"Weekly: one 25-question mixed timed set (30 min) across all blocks opened so far + the 66-item trap bank as a monthly full retest."}]}]},{"title":"Phase 2 — Mixed-speed training (weeks 15–30)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Alternate days: (a) 40-question mixed probability sprints at 75 s/question; (b) full-syllabus Statistics-I sectionals where probability is embedded among other topics — discrimination under interference is the real exam skill."},{"x":"Introduce the **90-second protocol** per MCQ: 10 s classify the trap-class → 40 s apply the known verdict/counterexample → 20 s eliminate → decide (answer if at least one option eliminated) → 20 s buffer. Practise the protocol until it is automatic.","k":"key"},{"x":"Ledger metric to track weekly: repeat-error rate (same trap-class twice). Target under 10% by week 24. Accuracy target on sprints: 85% by week 20, 90% by week 30."}]}]},{"title":"Phase 3 — Exam simulation (weeks 31–42)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Two full Statistics-I mocks weekly (80 Q / 120 min) under strict conditions, each followed the same day by a 90-minute autopsy: every error tagged, every guess audited (was the elimination real?), negative-marking discipline scored."},{"x":"Rebuild speed reserves: first pass $\\le90$ min answering all sure questions; second pass for the eliminate-and-decide pool; never a third pass."},{"x":"Weeks 39–42: taper new material to zero; only ledger, flashcards, the 66-item bank, and the cross-family tables of the distributions chapter."}]}]},{"title":"Phase 4 — Peak (final 4 weeks before the paper)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"Daily 30-minute recall of: closure table, limit lattice, mean/variance table, convergence hierarchy + four counterexamples, the three CF theorems, hypothesis tables of the LLNs/CLTs, and the paradox verdicts."},{"x":"Three final mocks, spaced 4–5 days, last one 7 days out. No mock in the final week; the final week belongs to the ledger only."},{"x":"Sleep is a study technique: memory consolidation of exactly this kind of discrete fact-lattice happens offline. Protect it."}]}]},{"title":"Non-negotiables (the whole plan in five lines)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"x":"PYQs before theory; theory as debrief.","k":"key"},{"x":"Every mistake becomes a ledger line with a trap-class and two retest dates.","k":"key"},{"x":"Counterexamples are carried as flashcards until they answer themselves in under 10 s.","k":"key"},{"x":"Timed practice at 75–90 s/question from week 15 onward, with the guessing arithmetic applied ruthlessly.","k":"key"},{"x":"The 66-item bank is retaken monthly; 58+ or the block reopens.","k":"key"}]},{"t":"key","x":"**Final word from the faculty desk.** The syllabus paragraph you are chasing — from Kolmogorov’s axioms to the central limit theorem — is a single connected story: measures $\\to$ variables $\\to$ distributions $\\to$ transformations $\\to$ generating functions $\\to$ convergence $\\to$ limit laws. The exam samples the story at its joints. Own the joints (every TRAP in this file), and the 2027 paper will read like a list of questions you have already answered."}]}],"src":"traps"}];
